<Layout pageTitle="Biohybrid Laboratory — Methods & Validation" description="Equations, parameter provenance, primary literature, native NEURON comparison and retained validation discrepancies for the Biohybrid Neural Interface Laboratory." compactHeader>
<article class="bh-methods">
<p class="bh-kicker">Scientific methods / {MODEL_VERSION}</p>
<h1>Biohybrid Neural Interface Laboratory<br/>Methods &amp; Validation</h1>
<p><a href="/simulations/biohybrid-interface/">← Open the laboratory</a> · <a href="#validation">Numerical evidence</a> · <a href="#references">Reference register</a></p>
<p>This laboratory calculates an electrically stimulated engineered neuron, propagation through its axon tract, conductance-based transmission to receiving cells, and ideal extracellular recordings. Every membrane color and trace comes from the compartment solution. The geometry includes synthetic branching, a hydrogel scaffold, explicit synaptic landing branches, a stimulating source/return pair and three recording contacts.</p>
<p><strong>The initial release is a canonical Hodgkin–Huxley active-cable benchmark at 6.3 °C.</strong> It is not fitted to human cortical neurons, cultured graft neurons, stem-cell preparations, or a particular commercial implant. Numerical agreement with NEURON verifies the specified equations and implementation; it does not establish biological prediction. The synthetic cells are representative geometric constructions, not reconstructed specimen morphologies.</p>

<h2>1. Evidence and architecture</h2>
<p><a href="#adewole2021">Adewole et al.</a> demonstrate optically controlled living electrodes with engineered neuronal aggregates and long axon tracts, including in-vitro stimulation/readout and rat implantation proof of concept. <a href="#struzyna2015">Struzyna et al.</a> support micro-TENN architecture and anatomical host integration. These studies motivate the living bridge but do not measure the parameters of this electrical relay. The model makes an explicit exploratory substitution: electrical volume-conductor stimulation and ideal electrical recording around a defined active-cable network.</p>
<p><a href="#brown2024">Brown et al.’s Science Corporation preprint</a> concerns a mouse cortical-surface optical microwell graft and behavioral reporting. The separate <a href="#scienceArchitecture2024">company architecture description</a> proposes broader electronics and scaling. The laboratory treats the microwell result as a named preclinical experiment, and future bandwidth, channel count and chronic performance as prospective claims. Passive cell wells are not electrical recording channels.</p>
<div class="bh-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Evidence class</th><th>Meaning in this laboratory</th></tr></thead><tbody>{Object.entries(EVIDENCE_CLASSES).map(([key,value])=><tr><td>{key}</td><td>{value}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>

<h2>2. Active membrane and axial current</h2>
<p>For compartment i, Vᵢ is intracellular minus extracellular potential. Current is positive outward across the membrane. The cable balance is:</p>
<pre>Cᵢ dVᵢ/dt = −Iᵢ,ion − Iᵢ,syn + Iᵢ,injected
              + Σⱼ gᵢⱼ [(Vⱼ + φₑ,ⱼ) − (Vᵢ + φₑ,ᵢ)]
Iᵢ,ion = ḡNa,ᵢ mᵢ³ hᵢ (Vᵢ − ENa)
          + ḡK,ᵢ nᵢ⁴ (Vᵢ − EK) + gL,ᵢ (Vᵢ − EL)
dx/dt = αx(V)(1 − x) − βx(V)x</pre>
<p>The rate functions follow the original HH model in NEURON’s absolute-voltage convention. Removable singularities at the activation-rate limits use analytic limiting values. The temperature factor is 3^((T − 6.3)/10), used as a benchmark convention; changing T does not produce a validated mammalian membrane model. All visible membrane-bearing compartments carry the stated capacitance and active channel densities. Ends are sealed. There is no myelin, ion depletion, adaptation current, plasticity, inhibitory cell type, or spontaneous input in this release.</p>
<p>Membrane areas are calculated from the actual equivalent cylinder lengths and diameters. With area in µm², capacitance in nF is area × 10⁻⁵ at 1 µF/cm², and a density in S/cm² becomes total conductance in µS by multiplying area × 0.01. Axial resistance uses cylinder cross-sectional areas and the two adjacent half-segments. Branch endpoints have explicit massless junctions, so a shared parent resistance is not independently duplicated for each child.</p>
<p>The soma is an equivalent electrical cylinder. Its displayed ellipsoid is a schematic surface; neurite diameters are enlarged ×2 for readability while centerlines and electrode positions retain their physical coordinate system. The 2D fallback also uses minimum pixel widths and marker sizes for legibility. The 3D scale bar gives length at the camera focus plane; perspective changes apparent scale with depth. No electrical parameter is calculated from a display enlargement.</p>

<h3>Time integration</h3>
<p>The solver performs a backward-Euler tree voltage solve with channel gates held fixed, followed by a Rush–Larsen update at the new voltage:</p>
<pre>x(t + Δt) = x∞(V) + [x(t) − x∞(V)] exp[−Δt / τx(V)]</pre>
<p>The Hines elimination solves the branched voltage system without dense inversion. This is a first-order split method, not an exact nonlinear solution. Initial gates are at the scalar equilibrium of the stated ionic model, giving a default rest of {f(numerical.default.diagnostics.restMv)} mV. Invalid settings, singular contact positions and unsupported voltage excursions cause a visible calculation error. The code does not silently clip voltages to hide instability.</p>
<p>A Web Worker calculates each complete experiment. Replay subsequently reads the saved voltage frames and measurements. Display slowdown does not alter biological time or numerical state. Default runs save {numerical.default.diagnostics.frames} frames at 0.05 ms intervals; spike times use interpolated crossings of the fine integration steps. The reduced mobile population has one graft and two host cells, with the same default time and spatial resolution.</p>

<h2>3. Host–graft synapses and interpretation</h2>
<p>Each anatomically assigned presynaptic branch has its own spike detector at 0 mV. An upward crossing schedules an event after the specified delay. The conductance kernel has a peak-normalizing factor A:</p>
<pre>g(t) = gpeak A [exp(−(t − tevent)/τdecay)
                  − exp(−(t − tevent)/τrise)]  for t ≥ tevent
I_syn = g(t) (V − E_syn),   E_syn = 0 mV</pre>
<p>The event is applied at the first solver step on or after its scheduled time, introducing at most one step of timing quantization. The postsynaptic voltage is still solved from its membrane and axial currents; a synaptic event does not force a host spike. Multiple inputs add conductance. Changing the number of functional targets or setting conductance to zero preserves the constructed axon geometry and its electrical load.</p>
<p>“Relay-consistent recruitment” means a host crossed threshold after an event at that same host. This temporal condition alone cannot exclude direct field activation at arbitrary settings. The laboratory separately counts early host spikes. The default disconnected counterfactual produces four graft soma spikes and zero host soma spikes; that paired calculation supports synaptic dependence for the default experiment. A nonzero recording in the disconnected experiment can still be volume-conducted activity from the graft.</p>

<h2>4. Electrode coupling and extracellular recordings</h2>
<p>Stimulation uses an explicit source and equal opposite return in an infinite, real, homogeneous, isotropic conductor. Each current disk is sampled at 48 equal-area quadrature sites: four radial annuli and twelve angles. The extracellular potential enters differences between connected cable nodes; electrode current is not substituted for soma current injection. Rectangular pulse edges use their exact overlap with each numerical step, preserving the two-phase charge balance.</p>
<pre>Point source: φₑ(r) = I / (4π σ |r − rsource|)
Line source:  φₑ(r) = I / (4π σ L) ∫segment ds / |r − r(s)|
Finite recording contact: Vcontact = (1/A) ∫disk φₑ(r) dA</pre>
<p>Recorded sources are the total outward transmembrane currents: capacitive, ionic and synaptic. Somata use the point-source approximation; neurites use the analytic line-source kernel shared with the Cortex Laboratory. Passive recording disks average those potentials with the same deterministic quadrature. Junctions have no membrane and contribute no source current. The reference is at infinity. The neural component, imposed-field artifact and their sum are separate quantities in the interface and CSV exports.</p>
<p>Finite-disk averaging is an approximate contact correction. It does not solve the conductive metal, an insulating substrate, electrode polarization, double-layer impedance, amplifier loading, filtering, noise or stimulus saturation. The displayed scaffold is not a separately solved conductivity region. There is no finite-element or electrodiffusion claim and no ephaptic feedback from the calculated neural field.</p>
<p>Contacts must stay at least 3 µm from the modeled membrane and from each other. A complete-disk geometric clearance calculation checks the membrane between quadrature samples. This exclusion prevents singular unsupported placements; it does not validate near-membrane physics. A point source obeys inverse-distance scaling, while a complete neuron’s multipolar signal can change non-monotonically with position. Inverse-conductivity validation holds the source currents fixed, because changing conductivity during stimulation can also alter firing.</p>

<h2>5. Parameter register</h2>
<div class="bh-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Default</th><th>Provenance / interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody>{params.map(([a,b,c])=><tr><td>{a}</td><td>{b}</td><td>{c}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
<p>The default conductance, morphology, sparse network and contact configuration are chosen to make an inspectable numerical experiment. They are not measured thresholds, an implant specification or a therapeutic recommendation. Every JSON export carries the normalized settings, model version, units, metrics and limitations of the completed run. Modified controls do not relabel previously calculated results.</p>

<h2 id="validation">6. Numerical verification and retained discrepancies</h2>
<p>Acceptance thresholds were documented before the final studies. Numerical tests cover equilibrium, zero input, spike initiation and propagation, length/diameter effects, synaptic transmission, disconnected behavior, amplitude/polarity, finite contacts, distance, conductivity with fixed sources, local current balance, deterministic replay, time and space refinement, parameter rejection and exported data integrity.</p>
<div class="bh-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Study</th><th>Measured value</th><th>Predetermined acceptance</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Zero-input drift, 40 ms</td><td>{f(checks.zeroInput.maxDriftMv,4)} mV; {checks.zeroInput.spikes} spikes</td><td>≤0.1 mV; no spikes</td><td class={checks.zeroInput.passed?'bh-passed':'bh-failed'}>{checks.zeroInput.passed?'Passed':'Failed'}</td></tr>
{temporalStudies.map(study=><tr><td>Time step {study.dtMs[0]} → {study.dtMs[1]} ms</td><td>Maximum monitored RMS {f(Math.max(...study.rmsByTrace.map(x=>x.rmsMv)),5)} mV</td><td>≤1 mV</td><td class={Math.max(...study.rmsByTrace.map(x=>x.rmsMv))<=numerical.acceptance.timeRmsMv?'bh-passed':'bh-failed'}>{Math.max(...study.rmsByTrace.map(x=>x.rmsMv))<=numerical.acceptance.timeRmsMv?'Passed':'Failed'}</td></tr>)}
{temporalStudies.map(study=><tr><td>Spike-time change, {study.dtMs[0]} → {study.dtMs[1]} ms</td><td>{f(study.maxSpikeShiftMs,5)} ms</td><td>≤0.1 ms</td><td class={study.maxSpikeShiftMs<=numerical.acceptance.spikeShiftMs?'bh-passed':'bh-failed'}>{study.maxSpikeShiftMs<=numerical.acceptance.spikeShiftMs?'Passed':'Failed'}</td></tr>)}
