<tr><td>25 → 12.5 µm segments: neural recording p–p</td><td>Largest change {f(100*spatialMax,4)}%</td><td>≤10%</td><td class={spatialMax<=numerical.acceptance.spatialRecordingRelative?'bh-passed':'bh-failed'}>{spatialMax<=numerical.acceptance.spatialRecordingRelative?'Passed':'Failed'}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Default global current residual</td><td>{f(numerical.default.diagnostics.maxCurrentBalanceNa,4)} nA</td><td>Current conservation to numerical precision</td><td class="bh-passed">Recorded diagnostic</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The temporal study uses the predeclared 0.005, 0.0025 and 0.00125 ms levels. Both adjacent comparisons use the same saved sample grid and matching per-neuron threshold-event counts. All seven monitored RMS differences decrease at the second halving; the coarse-to-half versus half-to-quarter difference ratios range from 1.65 to 2.10. This measures difference scaling for these discrete solutions, not error against an exact solution or proof of asymptotic convergence order. Nonlinear spike timing and delayed-event quantization contribute to the differences. Further spatial refinement to 6.25 µm also continues the trend. These are bounded comparisons at declared settings, not guarantees throughout every extreme parameter combination. Recorded runtime in the validation environment was approximately {f(numerical.default.diagnostics.elapsedMs/1000,3)} s for the default 40 ms calculation, excluding page and rendering startup. Browser hardware affects this measurement.</p>
<h3>Preserved failed spatial test</h3>
<p class="bh-failed"><strong>The initial spatial test failed.</strong> Recording 1 changed approximately 17.6% relative to the coarse result, or 21.3% relative to the refined result, exceeding the 10% gate. Selecting a synaptic target by its nearest discretized compartment could move it onto another anatomical branch during refinement.</p>
<p>The correction uses a fixed 6 µm dendritic landing branch, so the physical target stays fixed as segment size changes. The original failure, cause and correction remain in <code>docs/biohybrid/VALIDATION_HISTORY.md</code> and the numerical JSON report. The acceptance tolerance was not relaxed.</p>

<h3>Independent native NEURON comparison</h3>
<p>The reference uses NEURON {native.reference.version} with native HH channels, its cable solver, Exp2Syn, NetCon and membrane-current bookkeeping. Python independently rebuilds axial resistances and disk/line forward fields from the shared geometry and settings. The prescribed extracellular field is represented by mathematically equivalent nodal current sources, including the massless branch endpoints. This compares the defined discretized cable; it is not an independent tissue conductivity boundary solve.</p>
<p>No time shift, baseline correction or amplitude rescaling is applied before comparison. Frozen acceptance criteria are membrane RMS ≤1 mV, spike timing ≤0.1 ms, identical spike counts, and maximum extracellular error ≤0.001 µV. The stricter pointwise membrane criterion of ≤0.5 mV is a preserved additional diagnostic in the native report, reported separately from its acceptance flag. Its threshold has not been changed.</p>
<div class="bh-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Native case</th><th>Membrane RMS / mV</th><th>Maximum membrane error / mV; pointwise diagnostic</th><th>Spike-time error / ms; counts</th><th>Maximum neural recording error / µV</th><th>Acceptance result</th></tr></thead><tbody>{native.cases.map(c=><tr><td>{c.description??c.id}</td><td>{f(c.comparison.membraneRmsErrorMv,4)}</td><td>{f(c.comparison.membraneMaximumAbsoluteErrorMv,4)} · <span class={c.checks.strictPointwiseDiagnostic?'bh-passed':'bh-failed'}>{c.checks.strictPointwiseDiagnostic?'Passed':'Failed'}</span></td><td>{f(c.comparison.maximumSpikeTimingErrorMs,4)} · {c.comparison.spikeCountAgreement?'Counts match':'Counts differ'}</td><td>{f(c.comparison.extracellularMaximumAbsoluteErrorUv,4)}</td><td class={c.acceptancePassed?'bh-passed':'bh-failed'}>{c.acceptancePassed?'Passed':'Failed'}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
<p>The full membrane comparison includes Float32 replay quantization. Double-precision monitor traces and spike times agree more closely. Very close agreement between solvers at the same step does not remove the time-refinement error shown above; both implement the same stated finite-step convention.</p>
{native.retainedDiscrepancies?.length>0&&<div><h3>Reference discrepancies retained</h3><ul>{native.retainedDiscrepancies.map(item=><li>{typeof item==='string'?item:JSON.stringify(item)}</li>)}</ul></div>}
